## Overall Audit Summary

All five checklist items (CHK-0121 through CHK-0125) were analyzed with a complete three-phase formal audit. The key architectural finding is:

**The subgroup validation is correctly implemented in the c-kzg-4844 v2.1.1 C library** through `validate_kzg_g1` in `/home/gohan/go/pkg/mod/github.com/ethereum/c-kzg-4844/v2@v2.1.1/src/common/bytes.c` (lines 81-95), which calls `blst_p1_in_g1` from blst v0.3.14. This guard is applied to every commitment and proof byte before any group arithmetic in `verify_cell_kzg_proof_batch`.

**Findings by item:**

| Check ID | Final Classification | Bug Bounty Eligible | Key Finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| CHK-0121 | not-a-vulnerability | false | Pairing check never reached with non-subgroup points; `blst_p1_in_g1` blocks at deserialization |
| CHK-0122 | not-a-vulnerability | false | No bypass path exists; both batch and unbatched fallback paths enforce C library validation |
| CHK-0123 | not-a-vulnerability | false | No implicit trust assumption; C library always calls `validate_kzg_g1` unconditionally |
| CHK-0124 | not-a-vulnerability | false | Library correctly implements subgroup check; tested by c-kzg-4844 own test suite |
| CHK-0125 | not-a-vulnerability | false | Byte-level deduplication is mathematically equivalent to point equality for BLS12-381; semantics preserved |

**Architectural observations (not vulnerabilities but defense-in-depth gaps):**
1. The Go application layer (`VerifyDataColumnsSidecarKZGProofs` in `/home/gohan/runners/security-agent-2/_work/security-agent/security-agent/target_workspace/beacon-chain/core/peerdas/p2p_interface.go`) has no explicit application-layer subgroup check. This is intentional by design but means the entire cryptographic security of PeerDAS gossip validation depends on a single layer: the C library. A future vulnerability in blst's `blst_p1_in_g1` would have no defense-in-depth fallback.
2. The commitment deduplication for CHK-0125 is entirely inside the C library, which is the correct design. The Go code correctly avoids reimplementing this optimization.

